<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Die Lorelei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem <a href="/years/1824">1824</a>; Silcher's setting <a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lorelei is a steep slate rock, 132 metres high, on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the Rhine Gorge at Sankt Goarshausen. Its strong currents and loud echo made it a place of shipwrecks from the 10th century on, and its name is usually read as &quot;murmuring rock&quot;. Old tales placed dwarfs in caves inside it. The woman of the rock was the invention of a poet.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Lorelei is a steep slate rock 132 metres high on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the Rhine Gorge at Sankt Goarshausen, part of the Upper Middle Rhine Valley World Heritage Site. An amphitheatre of the 1930s stands on top of it. The place has been a site of river disasters since its first records in the 10th century, and the high number of shipwrecks and the loud echo in the passage gave rise to a varied mythology.</p><p>The name comes from the old German lureln, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> dialect word for murmuring, and ley, rock, so that it means murmuring rock. Heavy currents and a small waterfall, still visible in the early 19th century, produced a murmur that the echo of the rock amplified. Other theories derive the name from the verb lauern, to lurk, which would make it lurking rock. After the German spelling reform of 1901 the letter y was changed to i in almost all German words.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The rock and its murmur inspired various tales, and an old legend placed dwarfs in caves in the rock. The woman of the rock is a literary creation. In 1801 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clemens-brentano">Clemens Brentano</a> composed the ballad Zu Bacharach am Rheine, in which the beautiful Lore Lay, betrayed by her sweetheart, is accused of bewitching men and causing their death. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> sends her to a nunnery rather than condemning her, but on the way she asks to climb the rock to see the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> once more. Thinking that she sees her love in the river, she falls to her death, and the rock keeps an echo of her name. Brentano took inspiration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> and the myth of Echo.</p><p>In 1824 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-heine">Heinrich Heine</a> adapted the theme in his poem Die Lorelei, which describes a siren who sits on the cliff combing her golden hair and unwittingly distracts boatmen with her beauty and song, so that they crash on the rocks. Friedrich Silcher set it to music in 1837, and the art song became well known in German-speaking lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Lorelei of the Heine and Silcher song passed into German popular culture and is commonly but mistakenly believed to come from an old folk tale. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> also set the poem, as did dozens of other musicians. Joseph von Eichendorff's poem Waldesgespräch, from his novel of 1812, also features Loreley and was set by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a> in his Liederkreis. Under the Nazi regime Heine, who was born a Jew, was discredited as the author of the words in an effort to hide the Jewish contribution to German art. The French poet Guillaume Apollinaire took up the theme in La Loreley, which is cited in the third movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitri-shostakovich">Dmitri Shostakovich</a>'s Symphony No. 14.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lorelei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1824 Heinrich Heine took up Brentano's theme in &quot;<b>Die Lorelei</b>&quot;, one of his most famous poems.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li></ul></section>
